cially a progressive Democrat facing a well-funded Republican — could tighten or widen this margin.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["No expert ratings published yet; forecast rests on fundamentals and polls.", "New or vacant seat with no expert ratings; fundamentals only.", "No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings."]}, "overview": "Colorado enters the 2028 presidential race as a Democratic-leaning state, with a partisan lean of about six points toward Democrats and no incumbent on the ballot after the term-limited Republican president leaves office. No general-election polls or expert ratings are available yet, so the outlook rests on the state's fundamental lean.\n\nThe primary season has already made news: progressives defeated incumbent Democrats in Colorado primaries this summer, and early coverage suggests the Democratic ticket may skew left. On the Republican side, the field remains unsettled, with early straw polls showing no clear frontrunner. One published matchup found voters preferring a socialist-leaning Democrat over a MAGA-aligned Republican. The state's lean favors Democrats, but nominee choices on both sides could move the margin.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.7597, "margin": 6.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.6966, "margin": 5.5}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.7137, "margin": 6.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.701, "margin": 6.0}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.713, "margin": 6.0}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.7202, "margin": 6.0}, {"date": "2026-09-29", "p_dem": 0.7103, "margin": 6.0}, {"date": "2026-09-30", "p_dem": 0.7097, "margin": 6.0}, {"date": "2026-10-01", "p_dem": 0.7338, "margin": 6.5}, {"date": "2026-10-02", "p_dem": 0.7139, "margin": 6.0}]}